that's been very influential in computer vision as well.

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:17.040
I wanted to make this video optional because I tend to use the region proposal set of algorithms

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:23.120
a bit less often, but nonetheless, it has been an influential body of work and an idea that

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:28.000
you might come across in your own work. Let's take a look. So if you recall the

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:34.720
sliding windows idea, you would take a train classifier and run it across all of these different

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:40.080
windows and run a detector to see if there's a car, a pedestrian, maybe a motorcycle.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:45.520
Now you could run the algorithm convolutionally, but one downside of the algorithm is it just

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:51.680
classifies a lot of regions where there's clearly no object. So this rectangle down here is

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:57.040
pretty much blank. It's clearly nothing interesting there to classify. And maybe it was also running

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:01.200
on this rectangle, which looks like there's nothing that interesting there.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:06.400
So what Ross Gershwin, Jeff Donahoe, Trevor Darrow, and Jitendra Malik proposed in a paper

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:11.120
set to the bottom of this slide is an algorithm called RCNN, which stands for

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:17.920
regions with convolutional networks or regions with CNNs. And what that does is it tries to

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:23.760
pick just a few regions that make sense to run your confident classifier on. So rather than

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:30.000
running your sliding windows on every single window, you instead select just a few windows

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.520
and run your confident classifier on just a few windows.

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:39.920
The way that they perform the region proposals is to run an algorithm called a segmentation algorithm

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:46.080
that results in this output on the right in order to figure out what could be objects.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:52.160
So for example, the segmentation algorithm finds a blob over here. And so you might pick that

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:57.440
bounding box and say, let's run a classifier on that blob looks like this little green thing

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:02.480
that finds a blob there. So you might also run a classifier on that rectangle to see if there's

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:08.800
interesting there. And in this case, this blue blob, if it runs a classifier on that,

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:13.920
hopefully you'll find the pedestrian. And if you run it on this light cyan blob,

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:18.560
maybe you'll find a car maybe not not sure. So the details of this, this is called a

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:26.320
segmentation algorithm. And what you do is you find maybe 2000 blobs and place bounding boxes around

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:32.800
about 2000 blobs and run your classifier on just those 2000 blobs, which, and this can be a much

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:38.320
smaller number of positions on which to run your confident classifier than if you have to run it

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:43.520
at every single position throughout the image. And this is especially the case, if you are running

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:49.760
your confident not just on square shape regions, but running them on tall skinny regions to try to

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:54.960
find pedestrians, but running them on your white fat regions to try to find cars and running them

00:02:54.960 --> 00:03:04.240
at multiple scales as well. So that's the RCNN or the region with CNN or region of CNN features idea.

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:10.960
Now it turns out the RCNN algorithm is still quite slow. So there's been a line of work to explore

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:17.040
how to speed up this algorithm. So the basic RCNN algorithm with proposed regions using some

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:22.240
algorithm and the classified proposed regions one at a time. And for each of the regions,

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:27.520
they will output the label. So is there a car? Is there a pedestrian? Is there a motorcycle there?

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:33.840
And also output a bounding box so you can get an accurate bounding box if indeed there is a

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:40.480
object in that region. So just to be clear, the RCNN algorithm doesn't just trust the bounding

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:48.800
box it was given. It also outputs a bounding box, BX, BY, BHBW, in order to get a more accurate

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:55.440
bounding box than whatever happened to surround the block that the image segmentation algorithm

00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:01.040
gave it. So you can get pretty accurate bounding boxes. Now one downside of the RCNN algorithm

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:06.400
was that it's actually quite slow. So over the years, there have been a few improvements to the

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:14.640
RCNN algorithm. Roscoe Schwick proposed the fast RCNN algorithm and it's basically the RCNN algorithm

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:20.080
but with a convolutional implementation of sliding windows. So the original implementation

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:27.360
would actually classify the regions one at a time. So fast RCNN used a convolutional implementation

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:34.400
